stion um but  you you mentioned the word agency in in  the documents  and you know you're a little wary about  and i was  pleasantly surprised in your talk how  you didn't go down the pathway  that's almost predictable nowadays when  people talk about documents as in the  agency of the non-human of the document  and all of these kinds of things which  which is interesting theoretically in  many ways  but sometimes it  departs from  the actual material so much that you  kind of lose the story a little bit but  it does raise a question for me i'm  curious like what you think about  the kind of  literature and things going on about the  agency of documents and all that you  certainly you must be thinking about  some of that a little bit as you write  but you have a unique approach to it it  seems i wonder if you could articulate  the way you think about those things  yeah  no thank you so much for this question  it's it is something i'm considering  but ultimately i think my whole project  is kind of based on  the power of reading and the power of  reading and interpreting  and changing value in the information  that people have  collected and read in the past  um which kind of goes into  you know  my writing about mediation  that  you know  [Music]  these documents don't i  i think that  it's really the people who are kind of  behind the documents who have the most  agency and yet are not exactly talked  about and  particularly in the context of the voc  um  so  you know it's  yeah i  i haven't really  mentioned the agency of archival  documents so much as the agency of the  clerks and the archivists who are  actively  compiling information and organizing  that information  and of course along the way the  transformation  that happens as they reinterpret or  selectively extract  shorter passages from longer resolutions  there  there are decisions being made that i  don't think that historians have fully  considered when they read their  historical documents  for examp