ted very  much  in the 18th century the shift from well  it's not a complete shift because these  resolutions are still being written by  date  it's just that now they have this  massive index to  be able to organize that  information um  [Music]  but  yeah it's certainly um  kind of the way of thinking and there  isn't just one index this is kind of a  a process  um  that you know spans most of the 18th  century so there are multiple registers  which are then later compiled and  printed in in the 19th  and the 20th century so um  [Music]  and you know in the 19th century print  edition  they will say oh this is for  students who might be curious about  the history of the voc so students can  actually use the index  to search for i guess thesis topics or  something  and just to see if there are enough  documents about that one topic so it is  definitely kind of a major shift  in thinking about how to research  the vocs past  i think if we there's no further  questions i'm going to bring the talk to  a close um so i'll stop the recording  now and i'll leave the window open for a  few more minutes for informal  conversation after that but please  everyone join me in uh thanking eugenie  you for such a fascinating talk and  wonderful q a so thank you
 

